There are many situations when transferring files from or to a target system is necessary.

Understanding different ways to perfrom file transfers and how networks operate can help us accomplish our goals during an assessment. We mist be aware of host features to prevent these behaviour, like application whitelisting or AV/EDR blocking specific applications or activities. File transfers are also affected by network devices such as Firewalls, IDS or IPS which can monitor or block particular ports or uncommon operations

one way we can transfer smaller files is with base64 encode and decode. if we have access to a terminal, we can simply encode the file to a string, then decode it on our machine (as we can simply copy paste strings)

we can encode with a command like:
cat <filename> |base64 -w 0;echo

then, copy paste the encoded file to something like the base64 decrypt website, or we can decode it in our cli, with:

echo <encoded-text> | base64 --decode

most companies allow outbound HTTP and HTTPS traffic through the firewall to allow employee productivity. Leveraging these transportation methods for file transfer operations is very convenient. 

PowerShell offers many file transfer options. In any version of PowerShell, the System.Net.WebClient class can be used to download a file over HTTP, HTTPS, or FTP.

OpenRead		Returns the data from a resource as a Stream(sequence of bytes)
OpenReadAsync		Returns the data from a resource without blocking the calling thread
DownloadData		Downloads data from a resource and returns a byte array
DownloadDataAsync	^^^^^^ without blocking the calling thread
DownloadFile		Downloads data from a resource to a local file
DownloadFileAsync	^^^^^^ without blocking the calling thread
DownloadString		Downloads a string from a resource and returns a string
DownloadStringAsync	without blocking the calling thread

EXAMPLES:

(New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadFile('<Target File URL'>, '<output file name and path>')

(New-Object Net.WebClient).DownloadFileAsync('<Target File URL>','<Output File Name and Path>')

