Protected file transfers
in order to protect our clients, we will have to use much more secure data transfer methods if certain conditions arise. This requires us to work around the eventuality that services like SSH, SFTP, and HTTPS being unuseable to use, but we still need a safe way to exfiltrate data, so we will do the following:

File Encryption on Windows
one of the best tools for secure file transfers from windows is Invoke-AESEncryption.ps1, an example would be:
Invoke-AESEncryption -Mode Encrypt -Key "<password>" -Text "I secretly cheated on a test once."

This will encrypt the anything given with the text argument using the supplied password. once exfiltrated, we can decrypt the data with the same tool, simply using the decrypt mode instead of the encrypt mode

on our target, we need to import the module in order to use it freely, this can be done with the command:
Import-Module .\Invoke-AESEncryption.ps1

File encryption on linux

OpenSSL is frequently included on linux distributions, and we can use it to encrypt and share encrypted files.

example encryption:
openssl enc -aes256 -iter 100000 -pbkdf2 -in /etc/passwd -out passwd.enc

example decryption:
openssl enc -d -aes256 -iter 100000 -pbkdf2 -in passwd.enc -out passwd


Catching Files over HTTP/S

in order to set up a webserver that supports encryption, we would need to use Nginx or Apache in order to make a safe to use webserver that supports encryption.


Nginx - Enabling PUT

A good alternative for transferring files to Apache is Nginx because the configuration is less complicated, and the module system does not lead to security issues as Apache can.

When allowing HTTP uploads, it is critical to be 100% positive that users cannot upload web shells and execute them. Apache makes it easy to shoot ourselves in the foot with this, as the PHP module loves to execute anything ending in PHP. Configuring Nginx to use PHP is nowhere near as simple.
Create a Directory to Handle Uploaded Files

        shellsession
User@htb[/htb]$ sudo mkdir -p /var/www/uploads/SecretUploadDirectory

Change the Owner to www-data

        shellsession
User@htb[/htb]$ sudo chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/uploads/SecretUploadDirectory

Create Nginx Configuration File

Create the Nginx configuration file by creating the file /etc/nginx/sites-available/upload.conf with the contents:

        shellsession
server {
    listen 9001;
    
    location /SecretUploadDirectory/ {
        root    /var/www/uploads;
        dav_methods PUT;
    }
}

Symlink our Site to the sites-enabled Directory

        shellsession

User@htb[/htb]$ sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/upload.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

Start Nginx

        shellsession
User@htb[/htb]$ sudo systemctl restart nginx.service

If we get any error messages, check /var/log/nginx/error.log. If using Pwnbox, we will see port 80 is already in use.
Verifying Errors

        shellsession
User@htb[/htb]$ tail -2 /var/log/nginx/error.log

